{"reason": "The meta-learner is severely overfitting (val log loss 1.137 vs DistilBERT 0.403). Historical experiment shows that removing high-variance tabular features and increasing regularization (dropout, weight decay, reduced hidden size) successfully fixed meta-learner overfitting. Module-level changes: 'model_design' to adjust meta-learner architecture and remove tabular features; 'training_evaluation' to update regularization hyperparameters and early stopping.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "WHAT: Modify MetaNet class to accept input_dim without tabular features (only 512-d embeddings + 6 probabilities = 518), reduce hidden_dim from 256 to 128, and increase dropout from 0.3/0.2 to 0.5/0.3. WHY: Removing the 20 noisy tabular features eliminates a major overfitting source. Reducing capacity and increasing dropout prevent memorization of training noise. HOW: Update MetaNet __init__ parameters, keep architecture otherwise identical (3-layer MLP, BatchNorm, GELU). Interface changes: constructor signature becomes MetaNet(input_dim=518, hidden_dim=128, num_classes=3). Ensure meta_train_features construction excludes tabular columns.", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Set meta-learner weight_decay to 0.05, early stopping patience to 5 epochs, max epochs to 50 (from 100). Use lower learning rate (5e-4). WHY: Higher weight decay and stricter early stopping control overfitting, as demonstrated in similar experiment. Reduced max epochs matches limited training need. HOW: Modify meta_optimizer weight_decay=0.05, meta_patience=5, change meta_net training loop to stop at 50 epochs. Keep scheduler (CosineAnnealingLR with T_max=50) and batch sizes unchanged."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The meta-learner is severely overfitting (val log loss 1.137 vs DistilBERT 0.403). Historical experiment shows that removing high-variance tabular features and increasing regularization (dropout, weight decay, reduced hidden size) successfully fixed meta-learner overfitting. Module-level changes: 'model_design' to adjust meta-learner architecture and remove tabular features; 'training_evaluation' to update regularization hyperparameters and early stopping.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "WHAT: Modify MetaNet class to accept input_dim without tabular features (only 512-d embeddings + 6 probabilities = 518), reduce hidden_dim from 256 to 128, and increase dropout from 0.3/0.2 to 0.5/0.3. WHY: Removing the 20 noisy tabular features eliminates a major overfitting source. Reducing capacity and increasing dropout prevent memorization of training noise. HOW: Update MetaNet __init__ parameters, keep architecture otherwise identical (3-layer MLP, BatchNorm, GELU). Interface changes: constructor signature becomes MetaNet(input_dim=518, hidden_dim=128, num_classes=3). Ensure meta_train_features construction excludes tabular columns.",
    "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Set meta-learner weight_decay to 0.05, early stopping patience to 5 epochs, max epochs to 50 (from 100). Use lower learning rate (5e-4). WHY: Higher weight decay and stricter early stopping control overfitting, as demonstrated in similar experiment. Reduced max epochs matches limited training need. HOW: Modify meta_optimizer weight_decay=0.05, meta_patience=5, change meta_net training loop to stop at 50 epochs. Keep scheduler (CosineAnnealingLR with T_max=50) and batch sizes unchanged."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The current model overfits severely (train loss 0.0009, val log loss 0.3666 at best epoch 2 then degrades). The initial plan proposes adding a CNN branch for n-gram features, progressive unfreezing, and extended context. Historical experiments show that adding a CNN branch and progressive unfreezing successfully reduced overfitting in similar author attribution tasks. While data processing and feature engineering could be improved, the most impactful changes are in model architecture and training strategy. The historical experiment with DistilBERT + ensemble showed overfitting was best tackled by architectural regularization and careful training schedules. Therefore, I focus on 'model_design' (add CNN branch with gating) and 'training_evaluation' (progressive unfreezing with discriminative learning rates) as the two most promising modules.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "WHAT: Add a parallel CNN branch that operates on token-level embeddings from ModernBERT's hidden states (last layer) before the classification head. The branch consists of three parallel 1D convolutional layers with kernel sizes [2,3,4], each producing 128 feature maps, followed by global max pooling and concatenation. The CNN features are fused with the [CLS] token representation via a learned gating mechanism (sigmoid-activated weighted average). The fused representation passes through a 256-unit hidden layer with GeLU activation, LayerNorm, and dropout 0.3 before the output layer. Retain the original classification head as an alternative fusion for the final logits. WHY: The CNN branch captures local n-gram patterns that BERT's deep self-attention may miss, providing a complementary inductive bias that reduces overfitting (as seen in historical experiments with mixed architecture ensembles). The gating mechanism allows the model to dynamically weight global vs local features. HOW: Implement a new class 'CustomModernBert' that wraps ModernBertForSequenceClassification, adding three Conv1d layers and necessary pooling/fusion layers. The forward method extracts last hidden states, computes CNN features, concatenates with CLS token, applies gating, and passes through the final classifier head. Preserve the original 'model' variable name and interface (forward returns logits). Use functional style for dropout/layernorm to maintain compatibility.", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Implement progressive unfreezing with discriminative learning rates. In epoch 1, freeze all BERT parameters (set requires_grad=False) and train only the newly added CNN branch and classification head with learning rate 1.5e-5. In epoch 2, unfreeze the top 4 BERT layers and set their learning rate to 1e-5 while maintaining 1.5e-5 for the head/CNN. From epoch 3 onward, fully unfreeze all layers with layer-wise learning rate decay: a base learning rate of 5e-6 for bottom layers linearly increasing to 1e-5 for top layers. Use separate parameter groups in the optimizer with different weight decay (0.01 for all). Keep gradient accumulation steps=2, batch size=8, warmup ratio=0.1, gradient clipping=1.0, early stopping patience=3, and validation procedures unchanged. WHY: Progressive unfreezing allows the CNN branch to learn stable features before fine-tuning BERT, preventing the newly initialized head from competing with pre-trained weights (as seen in historical experiment where blind fine-tuning led to overfitting). Discriminative learning rates preserve pre-trained BERT knowledge by applying lower learning rates to lower layers. HOW: Modify the optimizer creation to use parameter groups. In each epoch, adjust requires_grad flags and recreate the optimizer/scheduler with appropriate learning rates for each group. Keep the training loop structure and validation metrics (log loss, accuracy) unchanged. Use model.named_parameters() to identify layers by name for freezing/unfreezing."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current model overfits severely (train loss 0.0009, val log loss 0.3666 at best epoch 2 then degrades). The initial plan proposes adding a CNN branch for n-gram features, progressive unfreezing, and extended context. Historical experiments show that adding a CNN branch and progressive unfreezing successfully reduced overfitting in similar author attribution tasks. While data processing and feature engineering could be improved, the most impactful changes are in model architecture and training strategy. The historical experiment with DistilBERT + ensemble showed overfitting was best tackled by architectural regularization and careful training schedules. Therefore, I focus on 'model_design' (add CNN branch with gating) and 'training_evaluation' (progressive unfreezing with discriminative learning rates) as the two most promising modules.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "WHAT: Add a parallel CNN branch that operates on token-level embeddings from ModernBERT's hidden states (last layer) before the classification head. The branch consists of three parallel 1D convolutional layers with kernel sizes [2,3,4], each producing 128 feature maps, followed by global max pooling and concatenation. The CNN features are fused with the [CLS] token representation via a learned gating mechanism (sigmoid-activated weighted average). The fused representation passes through a 256-unit hidden layer with GeLU activation, LayerNorm, and dropout 0.3 before the output layer. Retain the original classification head as an alternative fusion for the final logits. WHY: The CNN branch captures local n-gram patterns that BERT's deep self-attention may miss, providing a complementary inductive bias that reduces overfitting (as seen in historical experiments with mixed architecture ensembles). The gating mechanism allows the model to dynamically weight global vs local features. HOW: Implement a new class 'CustomModernBert' that wraps ModernBertForSequenceClassification, adding three Conv1d layers and necessary pooling/fusion layers. The forward method extracts last hidden states, computes CNN features, concatenates with CLS token, applies gating, and passes through the final classifier head. Preserve the original 'model' variable name and interface (forward returns logits). Use functional style for dropout/layernorm to maintain compatibility.",
    "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Implement progressive unfreezing with discriminative learning rates. In epoch 1, freeze all BERT parameters (set requires_grad=False) and train only the newly added CNN branch and classification head with learning rate 1.5e-5. In epoch 2, unfreeze the top 4 BERT layers and set their learning rate to 1e-5 while maintaining 1.5e-5 for the head/CNN. From epoch 3 onward, fully unfreeze all layers with layer-wise learning rate decay: a base learning rate of 5e-6 for bottom layers linearly increasing to 1e-5 for top layers. Use separate parameter groups in the optimizer with different weight decay (0.01 for all). Keep gradient accumulation steps=2, batch size=8, warmup ratio=0.1, gradient clipping=1.0, early stopping patience=3, and validation procedures unchanged. WHY: Progressive unfreezing allows the CNN branch to learn stable features before fine-tuning BERT, preventing the newly initialized head from competing with pre-trained weights (as seen in historical experiment where blind fine-tuning led to overfitting). Discriminative learning rates preserve pre-trained BERT knowledge by applying lower learning rates to lower layers. HOW: Modify the optimizer creation to use parameter groups. In each epoch, adjust requires_grad flags and recreate the optimizer/scheduler with appropriate learning rates for each group. Keep the training loop structure and validation metrics (log loss, accuracy) unchanged. Use model.named_parameters() to identify layers by name for freezing/unfreezing."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan identifies meta-learner overfitting as the root cause (val log loss 1.043 vs DistilBERT 0.392). Similar historical experiments show that removing noisy tabular features and increasing regularization (dropout, weight decay) successfully fixed meta-learner overfitting. Additionally, improving DistilBERT's text encoder via attention pooling and adding text augmentation can further enhance embedding quality. These changes target the 'model_design' module for architecture improvements in both the text encoder and meta-learner, and the 'training_evaluation' module for adjusted training strategies.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "WHAT: Replace mean pooling in DistilBERT text encoder with trainable attention-weighted pooling (CLS + multi-head attention over token hidden states). Modify MetaNet to reduce input dimension by excluding tabular features (input_dim=512+6=518), reduce hidden_dim from 128 to 64, and increase dropout to 0.5/0.3. Additionally, add text augmentation in DistilBERT training via random word dropout and synonym replacement using WordNet or a predefined dictionary. WHY: Attention pooling extracts more discriminative features from DistilBERT, reducing noise for the meta-learner. Removing tabular features and reducing capacity addresses meta-learner overfitting seen in historical experiments. Text augmentation reduces overfitting of DistilBERT. HOW: Implement attention pooling by mean-pooling token hidden states, applying multi-head attention with CLS token as query, and using weighted sum. For MetaNet, modify constructor to accept input_dim without tabular features and adjust architecture. For augmentation, create a helper function that randomly drops words (5% probability) and replaces words with synonyms (3% probability) using WordNet or a fixed list. Interface: TextFeatureEncoder's forward signature remains (input_ids, attention_mask, return_embeddings=False); MetaNet's __init__ remains MetaNet(input_dim, hidden_dim, num_classes).", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Add gradient clipping (max_norm=1.0) to meta-learner training, switch meta-learner scheduler to OneCycleLR with max_lr=5e-4 and total_steps=ceil(len(meta_train_loader)*100), increase meta-learner max epochs to 100 with early stopping patience of 10. For DistilBERT, reduce learning rate for classifier head to 1e-4 and add dropout (0.3->0.4) and weight decay (0.01->0.05) to reduce overfitting. WHY: Meta-learner early stops at 6 epochs, far too few for convergence. Gradient clipping prevents exploding gradients from concatenated features. OneCycleLR with longer training allows proper convergence. Stronger regularization for DistilBERT aligns with historical success in controlling overfitting. HOW: Modify meta_optimizer = AdamW(..., weight_decay=0.05), add torch.nn.utils.clip_grad_norm_(meta_net.parameters(), max_norm=1.0) after loss.backward(). Replace CosineAnnealingLR with OneCycleLR. Increase patience to 10, max epochs to 100. For DistilBERT, adjust optimizer learning rates and add weight_decay=0.05. Interface: all training loop variable names remain unchanged; only hyperparameters modified."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan identifies meta-learner overfitting as the root cause (val log loss 1.043 vs DistilBERT 0.392). Similar historical experiments show that removing noisy tabular features and increasing regularization (dropout, weight decay) successfully fixed meta-learner overfitting. Additionally, improving DistilBERT's text encoder via attention pooling and adding text augmentation can further enhance embedding quality. These changes target the 'model_design' module for architecture improvements in both the text encoder and meta-learner, and the 'training_evaluation' module for adjusted training strategies.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "WHAT: Replace mean pooling in DistilBERT text encoder with trainable attention-weighted pooling (CLS + multi-head attention over token hidden states). Modify MetaNet to reduce input dimension by excluding tabular features (input_dim=512+6=518), reduce hidden_dim from 128 to 64, and increase dropout to 0.5/0.3. Additionally, add text augmentation in DistilBERT training via random word dropout and synonym replacement using WordNet or a predefined dictionary. WHY: Attention pooling extracts more discriminative features from DistilBERT, reducing noise for the meta-learner. Removing tabular features and reducing capacity addresses meta-learner overfitting seen in historical experiments. Text augmentation reduces overfitting of DistilBERT. HOW: Implement attention pooling by mean-pooling token hidden states, applying multi-head attention with CLS token as query, and using weighted sum. For MetaNet, modify constructor to accept input_dim without tabular features and adjust architecture. For augmentation, create a helper function that randomly drops words (5% probability) and replaces words with synonyms (3% probability) using WordNet or a fixed list. Interface: TextFeatureEncoder's forward signature remains (input_ids, attention_mask, return_embeddings=False); MetaNet's __init__ remains MetaNet(input_dim, hidden_dim, num_classes).",
    "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Add gradient clipping (max_norm=1.0) to meta-learner training, switch meta-learner scheduler to OneCycleLR with max_lr=5e-4 and total_steps=ceil(len(meta_train_loader)*100), increase meta-learner max epochs to 100 with early stopping patience of 10. For DistilBERT, reduce learning rate for classifier head to 1e-4 and add dropout (0.3->0.4) and weight decay (0.01->0.05) to reduce overfitting. WHY: Meta-learner early stops at 6 epochs, far too few for convergence. Gradient clipping prevents exploding gradients from concatenated features. OneCycleLR with longer training allows proper convergence. Stronger regularization for DistilBERT aligns with historical success in controlling overfitting. HOW: Modify meta_optimizer = AdamW(..., weight_decay=0.05), add torch.nn.utils.clip_grad_norm_(meta_net.parameters(), max_norm=1.0) after loss.backward(). Replace CosineAnnealingLR with OneCycleLR. Increase patience to 10, max epochs to 100. For DistilBERT, adjust optimizer learning rates and add weight_decay=0.05. Interface: all training loop variable names remain unchanged; only hyperparameters modified."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "Based on initial plan and historical experiments: The current code achieves a validation log-loss of 0.48 using only handcrafted features with XGBoost/LightGBM. The initial plan proposed adding a DistilBERT model to capture semantic patterns orthogonal to surface features. Historical experiment #2 (successful) used a similar DistilBERT + XGBoost + LightGBM ensemble with a meta-learner, achieving better performance. However, experiment #1 failed due to overfitting (log-loss 1.137). The key refinement is to replace the meta-learner with simple weighted averaging to avoid overfitting, as the small dataset (17k samples) makes complex meta-learners risky. Also, historical experiment #2 showed that handcrafted features (readability, POS, thematic words) are crucial - they should be kept unchanged. The modifications focus on: 1) model_design to integrate DistilBERT and define weighted ensemble, 2) training_evaluation to implement fine-tuning and ensemble prediction, while keeping data_processing_and_feature_engineering unchanged as it already works well.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Integrate a DistilBERT model (distilbert-base-uncased) as a new classifier alongside existing XGBoost and LightGBM. The DistilBERT model will tokenize text with a max length of 128, use a dropout of 0.2, and output logits for 3 authors. The ensemble will combine predictions via weighted averaging: XGBoost weight=0.25, LightGBM weight=0.25, DistilBERT weight=0.5 (adjustable via grid search on validation set). This addresses the initial plan's need for deep contextual representations, while learning from historical experiment #2's successful use of DistilBERT but avoiding its overcomplicated meta-learner (which caused overfitting in experiment #1). Implementation: define a PyTorch nn.Module for DistilBERT with classification head, using Hugging Face Transformers. Keep the existing model_design components unchanged (XGBoost/LightGBM hyperparameters).", "training_evaluation": "Implement a PyTorch training loop for DistilBERT: use AdamW optimizer (lr=2e-5), linear warmup schedule, batch size 16, train for 3 epochs with gradient accumulation (0-2 steps) if needed. Use the same stratified split (first fold) as existing code. Apply gradient clipping (max norm=1.0) to prevent loss spikes. Evaluate on validation set after each epoch and save best model. Then generate predictions from DistilBERT on test set. Modify ensemble section to include DistilBERT test predictions with the weights defined in model_design. Clip and normalize final probabilities (epsilon=1e-15). This approach avoids historical experiment #1's overfitting (log-loss 1.137) by using simple weighted averaging instead of a meta-learner, and leverages the successful DistilBERT fine-tuning approach from experiment #2. Output submission file to ./submission/submission.csv as required."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "Based on initial plan and historical experiments: The current code achieves a validation log-loss of 0.48 using only handcrafted features with XGBoost/LightGBM. The initial plan proposed adding a DistilBERT model to capture semantic patterns orthogonal to surface features. Historical experiment #2 (successful) used a similar DistilBERT + XGBoost + LightGBM ensemble with a meta-learner, achieving better performance. However, experiment #1 failed due to overfitting (log-loss 1.137). The key refinement is to replace the meta-learner with simple weighted averaging to avoid overfitting, as the small dataset (17k samples) makes complex meta-learners risky. Also, historical experiment #2 showed that handcrafted features (readability, POS, thematic words) are crucial - they should be kept unchanged. The modifications focus on: 1) model_design to integrate DistilBERT and define weighted ensemble, 2) training_evaluation to implement fine-tuning and ensemble prediction, while keeping data_processing_and_feature_engineering unchanged as it already works well.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Integrate a DistilBERT model (distilbert-base-uncased) as a new classifier alongside existing XGBoost and LightGBM. The DistilBERT model will tokenize text with a max length of 128, use a dropout of 0.2, and output logits for 3 authors. The ensemble will combine predictions via weighted averaging: XGBoost weight=0.25, LightGBM weight=0.25, DistilBERT weight=0.5 (adjustable via grid search on validation set). This addresses the initial plan's need for deep contextual representations, while learning from historical experiment #2's successful use of DistilBERT but avoiding its overcomplicated meta-learner (which caused overfitting in experiment #1). Implementation: define a PyTorch nn.Module for DistilBERT with classification head, using Hugging Face Transformers. Keep the existing model_design components unchanged (XGBoost/LightGBM hyperparameters).",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement a PyTorch training loop for DistilBERT: use AdamW optimizer (lr=2e-5), linear warmup schedule, batch size 16, train for 3 epochs with gradient accumulation (0-2 steps) if needed. Use the same stratified split (first fold) as existing code. Apply gradient clipping (max norm=1.0) to prevent loss spikes. Evaluate on validation set after each epoch and save best model. Then generate predictions from DistilBERT on test set. Modify ensemble section to include DistilBERT test predictions with the weights defined in model_design. Clip and normalize final probabilities (epsilon=1e-15). This approach avoids historical experiment #1's overfitting (log-loss 1.137) by using simple weighted averaging instead of a meta-learner, and leverages the successful DistilBERT fine-tuning approach from experiment #2. Output submission file to ./submission/submission.csv as required."
